# Discovery and interpretation boundaries

Scope: declare a useful span using discovery and interpretation boundaries.

## When to use this type

Finding a candidate does not establish eligibility for strict interpretation or execution. Explicit inputs may legitimately follow stricter rules than discovered neighbors; preserve authorised differences.

## Information and anchors

State discovery scope, ownership predicate, strict validation boundary, ignore/error policy and exactly-once identity where relevant.

Candidate anchor roles: discovery promise; traversal/expansion; eligibility predicate; strict parser; mixed-content specimen. Select existing content carrying the rule or likely to be encountered at the risky decision. These are roles, not a required anchor count.

## Declare

1. Identify the risky local assumption and its authoritative rule; label inference or unresolved requirements.
2. Inspect existing spans before adding another. Choose anchors that expose the decisive information, including a single-anchor reminder when useful.
3. Select applicable distinguishing evidence: Combine a valid target, unrelated neighbor and malformed eligible target; check inclusion and deduplication using the actual identity rule.
4. Write the rule, scope and evidence limits in the why, then use the declaration recipe in [the skill](../../SKILL.md).

## Illustrative why

> Only marked declarations enter the publication grammar; unmarked helpers are ignored, while malformed marked declarations are errors.

This example supplies no authority for another repository. Substitute its actual contract and scope; quote decisive requirement wording where available.

## Adjacent types

Use [scope and identity](scope-and-identity.md) for deciding which candidates are the same entity; use [authority and precedence](authority-and-configuration-precedence.md) for deciding which eligible source wins.

Return to the [type selector](index.md) or [span guidance](../span-guidance.md) for authority, anchor delivery and test-reference rules. The suggested checks require separate execution; a span does not run them.
